EXT. TREEHOUSE IN A DEAD ELM - NIGHT

A plywood box wedged in the crotch of a bare elm. A string of Christmas lights, half burned out. Four sleeping bags laid in a square. Frost on the railings.

ANNELIESE KROLL, sixteen, sits cross-legged with a thermos. BO TRAN, sixteen, tunes a ukulele nobody asked for. KWAME BOATENG, seventeen, stretches on his back and stares up at a hole in the roof. SOLEDAD IBARRA, sixteen, perches at the edge with her feet in the air, rolling an unlit cigarette between her fingers.

BO
Okay, okay, this one's for the graduating class.

KWAME
We haven't graduated.

BO
It's for the future.

ANNELIESE
You can't even play it.

BO
I can play three chords.

SOLEDAD
Four if you count the one you broke.

Bo strums. It is awful. Kwame laughs into his sleeve. Anneliese passes the thermos to Soledad, and Soledad drinks and passes it on to Kwame, and it goes round without anyone speaking. The lights flicker. The ukulele fades.

BO
It's nice up here.

KWAME
Yeah.

ANNELIESE
Yeah.

Soledad says nothing. She rolls the cigarette once more, then sets it on the plank beside her and folds her hands.

SOLEDAD
Hey. Can I say something?

KWAME
Always.

SOLEDAD
I'm leaving in the morning.

Bo stops strumming. Anneliese puts down the thermos. Kwame sits up. The three of them turn to her at once, and the ukulele rolls off the platform and clatters through the branches.

ANNELIESE
What do you mean, leaving?

SOLEDAD
My dad got the job. In Tucson.

BO
Tucson?

KWAME
When?

SOLEDAD
Seven a.m.

Nobody speaks. Frost ticks on the rail. Kwame reaches across the square of sleeping bags and takes her sleeve between two fingers, and holds on. Anneliese climbs over Bo and puts her head on Soledad's shoulder. Bo just sits there, looking down at the dark where the ukulele went.
